<Base title="Understanding database performance" description="Trace a slow request through connection waits, generated queries, execution plans and maintenance, then test an improvement under a representative workload.">
 <meta slot="head" name="robots" content="noindex" />
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage · Working draft</p><h1>Understanding database performance</h1><div class="essay-meta"><span>Relational deep dive</span><a href="/learn/databases/relational/">Relational introduction ↗</a></div></header>
  <p>Database performance is the work a database can complete and the time its callers spend waiting for it. These are related, but a slow application request does not necessarily contain a slow query. It may wait for a connection, send dozens of individually quick queries, or pause behind another transaction before it can change one row.</p>
  <p>A query plan explains how the engine obtains an answer. To understand a slow service, we also need to follow the request that asked for it. Where did it wait? How many commands did it send? How much data came back? Once we know which part grew, we can make a change whose effect we can predict.</p>
  <p>We will follow an order-history endpoint and a background job queue. The timing examples are invented traces to make the diagnosis concrete. The SQL experiment runs in PostgreSQL through the browser sandbox; it measures row counts and plans, while a networked server is needed to investigate connections and competing requests.</p>
  <nav aria-label="In this article"><ol><li><a href="#request">The application request</a></li><li><a href="#waiting">Waiting inside the database</a></li><li><a href="#plans">Plans and changing data</a></li><li><a href="#maintenance">Maintenance</a></li><li><a href="#verification">Testing an improvement</a></li></ol></nav>

  <section id="request"><h2>The application request</h2>
   <p>The order-history handler needs a connection before it can send SQL. A <strong>connection pool</strong> keeps a bounded set of connections ready and lends them to handlers. When every connection is in use, a new handler waits for one to return. This wait happens before its first query reaches the database.</p>
   <p>Suppose a trace of one request divides its 180 milliseconds into these non-overlapping intervals. The query interval here is measured at the application and includes the journey to the server, execution and receipt of the result.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Illustrative request timing"><table><thead><tr><th>Part of the request</th><th>Elapsed time</th><th>What to investigate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Acquire a pooled connection</td><td>80 ms</td><td>Busy connections, how long handlers retain them, pool limits</td></tr><tr><td>Fetch the order headers</td><td>12 ms</td><td>One SQL command and its result</td></tr><tr><td>Fetch lines for ten orders</td><td>70 ms</td><td>Ten sequential commands taking 7 ms each</td></tr><tr><td>Build and send the response</td><td>18 ms</td><td>Application CPU, allocation and response size</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>Improving the order-header query from 12 to 2 milliseconds would save ten milliseconds here. Fetching all ten orders’ lines in one batch could remove nine request-and-response exchanges. Returning connections sooner could also help the handlers waiting outside the pool. The trace has exposed different changes with different reasons to work.</p>
   <p>An ORM can hide the ten line queries behind a property access such as <code>order.lines</code>. Record the generated SQL, its number of executions and the returned rows for one request. Keep sensitive parameter values out of ordinary logs, but preserve enough controlled diagnostic information to reproduce important differences, such as a customer with ten orders versus one with a million. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/request-cost/">application request article</a> develops batching and the mistakes that can follow from trying to put everything into one join.</p>
   <p>A bigger pool is not automatically the answer to a pool wait. It admits more simultaneous database work. If the server is already spending all its CPU or I/O capacity, extra connections can increase contention and make existing queries take longer. First inspect whether connections are doing useful SQL, sitting in open transactions, or being held while application code calls another service. Count the pools across all application instances: twenty connections in each of twenty instances permit four hundred sessions, before workers and administrative tools.</p>
   <p>Compare pool acquisition time with query duration at the application and with server observations. Keep their boundaries explicit. Adding a server’s execution time to an application query span would count that execution twice.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="waiting"><h2>Waiting inside the database</h2>
   <p>A query can reach PostgreSQL promptly and still spend most of its time waiting. Consider two checkout handlers changing the same stock row. The first updates the row, then calls a payment service while its transaction remains open. The second update finds the row quickly but cannot complete until the first transaction resolves. A better index will not release the first handler’s lock.</p>
   <p>On a PostgreSQL 18 server, an operator with permission to inspect activity can run this from a separate monitoring connection during the slowdown. Seeing details for other users normally requires elevated monitoring privileges such as <code>pg_read_all_stats</code>. This is a server investigation, not a multi-session experiment in the single-connection sandbox.</p>
   <Sql code={waits} label="Inspect active work and open transactions on a PostgreSQL server" />
   <p><code>pid</code> identifies the server process serving a connection. <code>wait_event_type</code> and <code>wait_event</code> describe a current wait; <code>pg_blocking_pids</code> identifies sessions blocking lock acquisition. Follow those process IDs to the application operation that owns them. If the stock update lists process 412 as a blocker and 412 is <code>idle in transaction</code>, the owner has stopped sending commands while retaining its transaction. Its <code>transaction_age</code> tells us how long that transaction has been open.</p>
   <p><code>query_age</code> is time since the active query began, or since the last query began for an inactive session. It is not a CPU-time counter. An <code>active</code> query can be waiting, and a missing wait event in one sample does not prove it has been using CPU continuously. Repeat observations and correlate them with the request trace. Take each sample in autocommit mode. If the monitoring tool has opened a transaction, end it between samples or call <code>pg_stat_clear_snapshot()</code> before sampling again: PostgreSQL can otherwise keep showing the activity information cached by that transaction. PostgreSQL documents these distinctions in its <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/monitoring-stats.html#MONITORING-PG-STAT-ACTIVITY-VIEW">activity view</a>.</p>
   <p>Different waits suggest different investigations. A lock wait leads to its blocker and transaction boundary. An I/O wait leads to the pages the workload needs and the storage serving them. A client wait may mean the server is waiting for the application to send another command or receive more data. An empty blocker array does not rule out those other kinds of waiting.</p>
   <p>For the checkout, shorten the period holding the stock lock by redesigning when the external payment occurs, while preserving the order and payment guarantees. Simply committing early changes those guarantees; <a href="/learn/databases/relational/transaction-boundaries/">transaction boundaries</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/conflicts-and-retries/">uncertain outcomes</a> explain the coordination needed. Cancelling an arbitrary blocker can discard useful work and may not repair the handler that keeps creating the problem.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="plans"><h2>Plans and changing data</h2>
   <p>Now suppose the request spends its time executing SQL rather than waiting for a connection or lock. An execution plan lets us ask where that work comes from: rows scanned, candidates rejected, repeated lookups, sorting, aggregation and page access. Start with the actual generated statement and representative parameter values. A different customer or date range can make a different plan sensible.</p>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=performance-estimates">changing-data lesson</a> prepares 10,000 jobs in a table called <code>perf_jobs</code>. Ten have state <code>queued</code>; the other 9,990 have state <code>done</code>. The only index initially enforces the job ID. We ask how many jobs are queued:</p>
   <Sql code={estimate} label="Run the count and inspect its execution plan after the lesson setup" />
   <p><code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> runs the query. The scan visits the table and emits the ten rows matching the filter; the aggregate above it turns those rows into one count. Read the scan’s estimated <code>rows</code> beside its <code>actual rows</code>, then look at <code>Rows Removed by Filter</code>: 9,990 in this starting state. The result has one row, but answering it required examining ten thousand.</p>
   <p>The lesson next changes 4,990 completed jobs back to queued without collecting new statistics. The count becomes 5,000. The stored distribution still describes the earlier population, so the planner underestimates how many rows the filter will pass. The answer remains correct: estimates choose a strategy; execution evaluates the actual records.</p>
   <p>Running <code>ANALYZE perf_jobs</code> updates the planner’s information. In this small experiment it can describe the new half-queued population accurately. A sequential scan can still be appropriate because the query needs half the table. Updating the estimate does not make those five thousand requested records disappear.</p>
   <p>The lesson then restores ten queued jobs and creates an index on state, finishing that batch before inspecting the next read. That gives the selective query a way to locate its few matches. Change the filter to <code>done</code>, however, and it needs nearly the whole population again. The useful question is how many entries and pages the chosen strategy visits for this request. “Uses an index” is not itself a performance result.</p>
   <p>Real data has less tidy distributions. A tenant may own most records; two filtered columns may be strongly related. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/planner-stats.html">statistics</a> include common values, distributions and optional statistics spanning multiple columns. Investigate where estimates first depart substantially from actual rows before changing statistics settings. A large mismatch near the bottom of a join plan can make the work above it unexpectedly expensive. Prepared statements also deserve testing in the application’s actual execution mode, because a plan shared between parameter values can differ from one made for a particular value.</p>
   <p>Interpret the measurements carefully. Plan cost is not elapsed milliseconds. Where a node has multiple <code>loops</code>, its actual row count is an average per execution. Buffer hits count accesses to pages already in PostgreSQL’s buffers; reads do not alone prove a physical storage access because the operating system may cache them. Parent measurements can include child work, so summing every node double-counts it. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-execution/">query execution article</a> follows these details through complete plans.</p>
   <p>A plain <code>EXPLAIN</code> shows the intended strategy without executing it. Use that distinction when investigating costly or modifying statements: <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE UPDATE ...</code> really performs an update. The experiment here uses a read on disposable records. Its browser execution times do not estimate a production server’s latency.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="maintenance"><h2>Maintenance</h2>
   <p>A database changes while it serves requests. New records alter distributions; updates leave old row versions behind; repeated scans compete for memory with other work. A query that was fast last month may have the same text and indexes but operate on a different physical and statistical state.</p>
   <p>In PostgreSQL, an update creates a new row version so existing readers can continue seeing an appropriate older one. Once old versions are no longer needed, vacuum can make their space reusable. It also maintains visibility information that can let an index-only scan avoid visiting table pages. <code>ANALYZE</code> collects planner statistics. They address different parts of the problem, although an automatic maintenance worker can perform both.</p>
   <p>Suppose the order table is updated heavily and grows much faster than its live row count. Check the rate of change, maintenance history and transactions retaining old snapshots. An old transaction can prevent cleanup of versions it might still need. Vacuuming more aggressively cannot reclaim those versions until that need ends. Conversely, ending the transaction makes cleanup possible; it does not instantly compact every page.</p>
   <p>Ordinary vacuum generally makes space available for reuse inside the table rather than returning all that space to the operating system. <code>VACUUM FULL</code> rewrites the table and takes a strong lock, so it is a different operational decision, not an interchangeable “stronger cleanup” button. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/routine-vacuuming.html">maintenance documentation</a> explains those behaviours. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/row-versions/">Row versions and vacuum</a> follows the visibility mechanism in detail.</p>
   <p>Observe maintenance during a sustained test. A freshly loaded table on an idle server can make both reads and writes look easy while omitting the old versions, checkpoint activity and competing jobs the application encounters after hours of use.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="verification"><h2>Testing an improvement</h2>
   <p>Before changing the service, state what should change and what must stay correct. For the order list, batching should reduce eleven SQL commands to two while returning the same ordered parents and children. For the queue, an index should reduce page work for a selective state. For the stock handler, shorter transaction ownership should reduce how long competing updates wait.</p>
   <p>Choose a disposable server environment with representative data volume, record width, skew, parameters and indexes. Send the application’s real read and write mix at a recorded arrival rate. Include the number of application instances and their pool limits. A test that issues the next request only when the previous one finishes lowers its own offered load during a slowdown; that can hide the queueing real traffic would create.</p>
   <p>Measure completed requests per second, failures, pool waits and latency distributions, including the slow end. A 95th-percentile latency of 200 milliseconds means 95 percent of measured completions took at most that long; the remaining requests may be much slower. Report timeouts separately rather than dropping them from the latency story. Compare query counts, rows and bytes transferred, server resource use and plans alongside the application measurements.</p>
   <p>PostgreSQL’s optional <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/pgstatstatements.html">pg_stat_statements</a> extension helps find frequently executed or cumulatively expensive statements. Compare counter differences over the same test interval; lifetime totals and mean execution times alone can hide a newly slow request or a rare expensive parameter. Enabling the extension requires server configuration, and it complements request traces rather than supplying their connection-pool waits.</p>
   <p>Run the baseline and candidate with the same workload, allow caches and background activity to reach the intended state, and repeat enough to distinguish a consistent effect from noise. Test large customers as well as average ones. Include writes when adding an index, and include report refresh when introducing a summary. If the change only moves the delay to another operation, that operation belongs in the result.</p>
   <p>Finally, observe a controlled rollout with a way to reverse the change. The investigation is useful when its prediction survives the service’s actual work: fewer exchanges for the order list, less access work for selective jobs, or shorter waits for checkout. A faster isolated query is evidence about that query; the endpoint and the competing workload decide whether the service improved.</p>
  </section>
